        "timestamp": 1774734959,
        "contenuto_esteso": "To display this content from YouTube, you must enable advertisement tracking and audience measurement. One of your browser extensions seems to be blocking the video player from loading. To watch this content, you may need to disable it on this site. Double fears over Red Sea, Hormuz Middle East Issued on: 28/03/2026 - 22:55 Modified: 28/03/2026 - 23:59 Worry continues to grow after Yemen’s Houthi rebels escalated their involvement in the conflict, with two missile fired toward Israel. The developments raise fresh fears that the group could again threaten Red Sea shipping routes, a vital global trade corridor, further increasing tensions across an already volatile regional conflict. Jean-Emile Jammine speaks with Kethevane Gorjestani, France 24's International Affairs editor, and Yousef Alshammari, President, London College of Energy Economics. Iranian strike on Saudi base wounds several US troops, damages aircraft 28/03/2026 Middle East What could Houthi involvement in the Iran war mean? 28/03/2026 Middle East 'History is repeating itself': Fears of Israeli occupation grow in Lebanon 28/03/2026 Middle East Ukraine, UAE seal defence deal: Kyiv’s 'know-how and equipment” gain recognition 28/03/2026 Middle East Israeli strike hits Tehran home, killing several children 28/03/2026 Middle East Beirut 'holding its breath' as Israel launches strikes across Lebanon 28/03/2026 Middle East Iranian-backed Houthi rebels say launched missile attack on Israel 28/03/2026 Middle East Yemen's Houthis claim missile attack on Israel in expanding conflict 28/03/2026 Middle East 'New phenomenon of dangerous rhetoric': World leaders embrace proud disregard for international law 27/03/2026 Middle East"
